SOAPBOX: My opening line from last year?s soap box was "Its fun to be loud!". This year, the headline is ?It?s frustrating to be weak!?
SOAPBOX: In December I experienced the 'Revenge of the Christmas Trees' when a large oak fell in our yard. It directly destroyed a few VHF antennas on it?s way to crush the steps to our deck, some railings and crack two skylights. The biggest impact to HF contesting was the loss of the mounting point for two of my wire antennas a 40M EFHW & a 10-20M fan dipole. I was able to re-mount the EFHW to another tree but only at about 15ft -
SOAPBOX: certainly not ideal. My other surviving HF antenna is a very low, but full size 80M dipole. My expectations were rather low.
SOAPBOX: I felt participation was good, with decent conditions on Fri. night through Saturday. Conditions on Sunday were not as good with many EU signals showing a "flutter" I usually only hear on over-the-pole stations and all stations generally weaker. 10M
SOAPBOX: was a dead zone on Sunday. I did manage to put over 700 Qs in the log but it was a long, tough slog. When I did try running I would only get a handful of Qs, if any at all. I did take advantage of the SO2R capabilities of my Flex 6600 when band
SOAPBOX: conditions allowed by calling a station on one side while searching the for next one on the other. It often required several calls and a few repeats to finish a Q. Many times I
SOAPBOX: could clearly read a station but they could not hear me. heavy sigh Honestly, I don't know how the QRP folks manage it!
SOAPBOX: One bright spot for me was a strong showing on 80M. I think this is the first 'test where 80M has made a significant contribution to my score. There were many stations active on 80 which is a refreshing change for me. Perhaps they were attracted by the point bonus available on the low bands - I sure was. I also appreciated the 30hr limit for SO stations. This allowed me to have some family time and get some decent rest through the weekend. I would support this rule for all SO classes in all contests. 48 straight hours of competition is on the raw edge of human endurance for the young and beyond that for those of us with more expansive 'life experience' - it is certainly far beyond mine!
SOAPBOX: One particularly frustrating pileup occurred on JT5DX, which would have been a RTTY ATNO for me. I could read him well but I could not cut through the surrounding bedlam. By this point I knew that I was not going to place well so I spent an inordinate amount of time trying to get the ATNO - No Joy.
SOAPBOX: 5 band contacts with EI7M, IO6T, P49X, SN7Q, SP8R and WP3C. 4 band contacts were made with 9A1A, AC0C, DA0BCC, DP6A, K9CT, LX7I, N4IQ, N5QP, NM4AA, OM5ZW, PJ2T, W0ZQ, WV4P and ZF2SS.
SOAPBOX: Despite the frustration and lack-luster performance I still enjoyed the event. Many thanks to the folks that keep the CQ contests running and of course, to all the ops for the patience to complete a Q with my moldy potato station!
SOAPBOX: 73 and see you in NAQP/RTTY in a few weeks!
